<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalmar War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1611">1611</a>–<a href="/years/1613">1613</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar">Kalmar</a> War was fought between Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from 1611 to 1613. Christian IV attacked to assert Danish dominance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and to stop Swedish expansion in the Arctic north. Danish forces took Kalmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvsborg">Älvsborg</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-knared">Treaty of Knäred</a> forced Sweden to pay a large ransom for Älvsborg, its only outlet to the west. It was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s last clear victory over Sweden.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalmar-war.jpg" alt="Kalmar War" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LocalF15 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalmar_War_Collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> controlled the strait between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, and the Sound Dues it collected there were its main source of income. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> sought an alternative trade route through sparsely populated Lapland to avoid paying them. In 1607 Charles IX of Sweden declared himself King of the Lapps in Nordland and began collecting taxes in Norwegian territory, even south of Tromsø. Christian IV of Denmark and Norway protested, and Charles ignored him. In April 1611 Denmark–Norway declared war and invaded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Swedes were ill-prepared, since their main field army was engaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingrian-war">Ingrian War</a>. The Danish and Norwegian plan was to attack on three fronts: from Kristianopel towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar">Kalmar</a>, from Halmstad towards Jönköping, and from the Norwegian side towards the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvsborg">Älvsborg</a>. A force of 5,200 Danish troops besieged and took Kalmar, and the Swedes had to scuttle some 20 ships in the harbour to keep them from capture. On 26 June a Swedish force carried out the storming of Kristianopel. In the summer Swedish forces under Baltzar Bäck took Jämtland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harjedalen">Härjedalen</a> with little fighting, but excesses against the population led to a rising, and the troops were forced out in the autumn of 1612.</p><p>Charles IX died on 20 October 1611 and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> sued for peace. Christian IV saw a chance of larger gains and strengthened his armies in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. In February 1612 Gustavus nearly drowned at the Battle of Vittsjö after a Danish army surprised him. In early 1612 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvsborg">Älvsborg</a> and Gullberg, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a>, leaving Sweden without access to the sea in the west. A thrust towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> failed against scorched earth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, and many unpaid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> deserted. Christian's naval expedition to Stockholm in August and September 1612 also failed, and he left the archipelago by 10 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, invested in Baltic trade, pressed for an end before a decisive victory could curtail <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s power. Christian, short of funds and reliant on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, agreed to talks, and with the intercession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James I of England</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-knared">Treaty of Knäred</a> was signed on 20 January 1613. Lapland was incorporated into Norway, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> had to pay a high ransom for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvsborg">Älvsborg</a> and Gullberg, two towns and six surrounding hundreds. Sweden won exemption from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> toll, with the right of free trade through the strait. Sweden recovered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a>, and in the Torstenson War Denmark–Norway lost and ceded control of the Baltic to the Swedish king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalmar War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Northern Seven Years’ War of 1563–1570 ended in stalemate at the Treaty of Stettin, and in the <b>Kalmar War</b> of 1611–1613 Christian IV took Älvsborg and forced Sweden to pay a heavy ransom for it at the Treaty of Knäred.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian IV won the <b>Kalmar War</b> of 1611–1613 but was beaten by Tilly at the Battle of Lutter am Barenberge in 1626 during the Thirty Years’ War, and the Torstenson War ended in the losses of the Treaty of Brömsebro in 1645.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Military</a></li></ul></section>
